{"name":"Tiāntāi Mountain Green","hex":"#566b38","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mountain","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An elevation of land of considerable dimensions rising more or less abruptly, forming a conspicuous figure in the landscape, usually having a small extent of surface at its summit. .mw-parser-output .defdate{font-size:smaller}","Something very large in size or quantity; a huge amount; a great heap."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mountain"},{"term":"green","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of grass and leaves; a primary additive color midway between yellow and blue which is evoked by light between roughly 495–570 nm.","A member of a green party; an environmentalist."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of a green hue.","Sickly, unwell."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/green"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mountain Green, Utah","blocks":[{"text":"Mountain Green is a census-designated place in northwestern Morgan County, Utah, United States. The population was 4,231 at the 2020 census. Located 16 miles (26 km) up the Weber River from Ogden, Mountain Green is the world headquarters of the Browning Arms Company."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/26/Mountain_Green_Utah_sign.jpeg/330px-Mountain_Green_Utah_sign.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mountain_Green%2C_Utah","wikidata":"Q9035432"},{"title":"Paris green","blocks":[{"text":"Paris green is an arsenic- and copper-containing pigment. It is an emerald-green crystalline powder that is also known as Mitis green, Schweinfurt green, Sattler green, emerald green, Vienna green, emperor green or mountain green. It is highly toxic and has thus also been used as a rodenticide and insecticide. Its formula is Cu(C2H3O2)2·3Cu(AsO2)2."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/78/Subunit_of_Cu2As3O3%28OAc%29_%28ASOCUA%29.png/330px-Subunit_of_Cu2As3O3%28OAc%29_%28ASOCUA%29.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Paris_green","wikidata":"Q339657"},{"title":"Tiantai Mountain","blocks":[{"text":"Tiantai Mountain is a mountain in Tiantai County, Taizhou, Zhejiang Province, China. Its highest peak, Huading, reaches a height of 1,138 meters (3,734 ft)."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/93/Sui_Dynasty_Guoqing_Temple_Ancient_Tower.JPG/330px-Sui_Dynasty_Guoqing_Temple_Ancient_Tower.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiantai_Mountain","wikidata":"Q1146118"},{"title":"Tiantai","blocks":[{"text":"Tiantai or T'ien-t'ai is an East Asian Buddhist school of Mahāyāna Buddhism that developed in 6th-century China. Drawing from earlier Mahāyāna sources such as Madhyamaka, founded by Nāgārjuna, who is traditionally regarded as the first patriarch of the school, Tiantai Buddhism emphasizes the \"One Vehicle\" doctrine derived from the influential Lotus Sūtra, as well as the philosophy of its fourth patriarch, Zhiyi, the principal founder of the tradition. Brook Ziporyn, professor of ancient and medieval Chinese religion and philosophy, states that Tiantai Buddhism is \"the earliest attempt at a thoroughgoing Sinitic reworking of the Indian Buddhist tradition.\" According to Paul Swanson, scholar of Buddhist studies, Tiantai Buddhism grew to become \"one of the most influential Buddhist traditions in China and Japan.\""}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/69/Mount_Tiantai_by_Wu_Bin%2C_1605%2C_Honolulu_Museum_of_Art_3678.1b.JPG/330px-Mount_Tiantai_by_Wu_Bin%2C_1605%2C_Honolulu_Museum_of_Art_3678.1b.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tiantai","wikidata":"Q668188"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg/500px-Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg/1280px-Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b7/Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg/500px-Mountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mountain Green Fire District","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMountain_Green_Utah_Fire_District.jpeg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG/500px-Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG/500px-Paris_Green_%28Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Can of Paris green pigment by Sherwin-Williams Co.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AParis_Green_(Schweinfurter_Gr%C3%BCn).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg/120px-Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg/250px-Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedicated_to_Gauguin.jpg/120px-Van_Gogh_self-portrait_dedi